# Public build configuration — the single source of truth for values that end
# up in the shipped HTML.
#
# THIS FILE IS COMMITTED ON PURPOSE. Everything in here is public by
# definition: it is served to every visitor inside the page source. Never put a
# secret here — those belong in `.env` / `.env.local`, which stay ignored.
#
# Why it exists (the full story is CLAUDE.md §3): a site is usually built by
# more than one machine — CI on a code deploy, and the CMS host after a publish
# if rebuilds are wired up. Both must produce the same site. When these values
# live in CI variables on one side and a server `.env` on the other, they drift,
# and the drift is silent: the page still renders, it has simply lost its
# analytics and — because the consent banner only appears when a service is
# registered — its cookie banner too.
#
# Astro loads this file for every production build (`astro build`) on every
# machine, so the values cannot disagree. Local `astro dev` is unaffected: it
# reads `.env`, which is why development gets no tracking.
#
# RULE: no build path may pass PUBLIC_* as an environment variable. An
# exported-but-empty variable silently overrides this file.

# CMS address that ends up in the HTML: image sources and the form endpoint.
# Deliberately NOT the build-time STRAPI_URL — a build on the CMS host fetches
# via 127.0.0.1, which in a browser means the visitor's own machine.
PUBLIC_STRAPI_URL=https://cms.example.com

# GA4 measurement id. The consent banner and the tracking code only exist in
# the build when this is set; nothing loads before the visitor opts in.
# Leave empty (or delete the line) for a site without analytics.
PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=
